}
```

### 2. Use Try-Catch Block
Wrap parts of your code that use `Scanner` inside a try-catch block to catch any potential errors and provide meaningful feedback.

```java
try {
    Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
    System.out.print("Enter your name: ");
    String userName = scanner.nextLine();

    System.out.println("Hello, " + userName + "!");

} catch (InputMismatchException e) {
    System.err.println("Error: Invalid input detected!");
    // Handle exception appropriately
} catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
    System.err.println("Error: No more inputs available!");
    // Handle exception appropriately
} finally {
    scanner.close(); // Always close the scanner to free up resources
}
```

### 3. Check User Input Format
Ensure that the input matches what you expect. For instance, if you need specific types of data (like integers), validate them before processing.

```java
